supramolecular binding of hydrocarbon molecules.10 Here we report a comprehensive investigation of the binding of light hydrocarbons within these two MOFs via neutron powder diffraction (NPD) and inelastic neutron scattering (INS), coupled with DFT modelling. These complimentary studies find that the hydroxyl protons play an important role in binding of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ molecules in the pores in contrast to the case of $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ where adsorbed $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ molecules are found to be remote from the bridging oxy groups. The pore environment of $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ is found to be optimal for binding of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and leads to an exceptional packing density of up to $0.38\ \mathrm{g\cm^{-3}}$ of adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at $303\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar.
## Results and Discussion
## Analysis of crystal structure and porosity of MFM-300(V)
$\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ was synthesised following our previously reported method10 and crystallises in the space group $I4_{1}22$ . Adjacent pair of $\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}}$ centres are bridged by two carboxylates and a $\mu_{2}$ -hydroxyl group forming an extended chain of $\mathrm{[V_{2}(O H)_{2}O_{4}]_{\infty}}$ along the $c$ axis. The carboxylate ligands further bridge the $\mathrm{[V_{2}(O H)_{2}O_{4}]_{\infty}}$ chains to give a three dimensional network with square-shaped channels decorated with hydroxyl groups pointing into the pores (Fig. 1). MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ was obtained by heating the as-synthesised $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ sample at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a flow of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for $16\mathrm{h}$ . MFM-300(VIV) retains the space group $I4_{1}22$ and the overall framework structure. The V-O bond length involving the bridging oxygen reduces upon oxidation, and loss of the hydroxyl hydrogen atom in MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ is confirmed. Analysis of the high pressure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ by DFT/Monte-Carlo methods gives surface areas of 1892 and $1565~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , pore size distributions (PSD) centred at 5.2 and $5.4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , and cumulative pore volumes of 0.490 and $0.481{\mathrm{~cm}}^{3}{\bf g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ and MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ , respectively. Another pair of MOFs, MIL-47(VIII) and MIL- $47(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ , has structural similarity to MFM-300(V). However, $\mathrm{{MIL}}{\cdot}47\mathrm{{(V^{III})}}$ and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}47(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ show significantly different porosity owing to framework flexibility as confirmed by $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption,11 and therefore cannot be directly compared in terms of host-guest binding. In comparison, MFM-300(V) adopts a rigid framework connection tied together by mutually linked cis- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot\mu_{2}$ -OH groups thus yielding highly robust isostructural frameworks for both $\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}}$ and ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{V}}}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ materials.
## Analysis of adsorption and selectivity of $\mathbf{CH_{4}}$ and $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ -hydrocarbons
Gravimetric adsorption isotherms for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ have been measured for both desolvated samples at 273-303 K (Fig. 2). At 1.0 bar, the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ are 8.1 and 6.9 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 273 and $303~\mathrm{K}$ , respectively, and those for $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ are 7.8 and 6.1 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ under the same conditions (Fig. 2b). The density of adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ is calculated to be $0.38~\mathrm{g~cm}^{-3}$ at $303~\mathrm{K}$ , which is 184 times higher than the safe compression limit for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage at 2.0 bar. This value is comparable to the highest value of $0.38\ \mathrm{g\cm^{-3}}$ observed in $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{2}(a d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]~[a d c^{2-}=9,10}}$ -anthracenedicarboxylate; dabco $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ 1,4-diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane] but at a lower temperature of $296~\mathrm{K},^{12}$ and higher than those for the benchmark MOFs, such as HKUST-1 $(0.31\ \mathrm{g\cm^{-3}}$ at $296\mathrm{K})^{13}$ and MOF-74(Fe) $(0.29\ \mathrm{g\cm^{-3}}$ at 296 K).14 It is interesting to note that at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ , the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ is 0.3 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ higher than that of $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ at 1.0 bar, while the difference is significantly greater $(\Delta=1.7\mathrm{\mmol\g^{-1})}$ in favour of $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ at 0.1 bar (Fig. S1). Given the similar pore volumes of these two samples, this result indicates the presence of much stronger host-guest interaction in MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ than that in MFM-300(VIV) at low surface coverage; with increasing gas loading, less surface sites are available and guest-guest interaction start to be significant in driving the gas adsorption, leading to a reduced difference in gas uptake.
The uptake capacities of $\mathrm{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbons of both MFM-300(VIII) and $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ track the order of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}>\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}>\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}$ at 1.0 bar and 273-303 K (Fig. 2). This is reasonable considering that gas uptake is inversely correlated with the molecular size at relatively high surface coverage. As for the uptake below 0.2 bar, gas uptake in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ follows the order of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}>\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}>\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , while that in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$
follows $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}>\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}>\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ . At low surface coverage, the effect of MOF-gas interaction is more pronounced in driving physical adsorption processes. The inversion of uptakes of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ and $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ indicates that the host-guest binding mechanism is very sensitive to the presence of hydroxyl groups in the pore and to the small difference in chemical structure of these hydrocarbon molecules.
The IAST selectivities of equimolar mixture of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{n}/C H_{4}}$ $(\mathfrak{n}=2,4,6)$ for $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ and MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ were calculated at $303~\mathrm{K}$ and 0-1 bar (Fig. 3). MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ outperforms MFM-300(VIV) for the selectivities of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C H_{4}}$ by ca. $40\%$ . This is likely due to the presence of stronger binding sites to unsaturated hydrocarbons in MFM-300(VIII). The $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}$ selectivity of $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ is between 22-14, and is nearly identical to that of $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ under same conditions. The $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}$ selectivity of MFM-300(V) is comparable with the benchmark MOFs, $\left[\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\right]$ (20 at $313\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1.0 bar)6 and UTSA-35a (15 at $296\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1.0 bar).15 Uptake capacities are of paramount importance in determining the performance of the pressure swing adsorption process, an energetically efficient method for industrial scale separations. The uptake for ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ in MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ is as high as $5.0\ \mathrm{mmol\g^{-1}}$ at $303~\mathrm{K}$ and 1.0 bar, which is comparable to that of MOF-74(Fe) $\left(5.0\mathrm{\mmol\g}^{-1}\right.$ at $318\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1.0 bar) and much higher than that of UTSA-35a (2.4 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $296~\mathrm{K}$ and 1.0 bar), suggesting that MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ is a promising material for separation ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .
## Analysis of isosteric heat of adsorption
The isosteric heat of adsorption, $\varrho_{\mathrm{st}}^{},$ for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ is centered at $32.0\pm0.1\mathrm{~kJ~r~}$ mol-1 without notable variation as a function of surface coverage. In contrast, the $\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ is estimated as $26.0\pm0.4\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ at low pressure and increases gradually with increasing gas loading to $30.1\pm$ $0.1\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ at $4.0\ \mathrm{mmol\g^{-1}}$ . Interestingly, the $\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ value for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ is significantly higher than that of MIL-53(Al) $(19.2\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}},$ ), which adopts similar structural feature but with trans-orientated hydroxyl groups at the metal centre.16 Indeed, this value is even higher than that of HKUST-1 $(30.4\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ )
incorporating open $\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}$ center.16 The $\varrho_{\mathrm{st}}$ values of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ for both compounds are similar and lie within the range of $28{-}32\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ . The $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ value of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ $(28\small{-}31\pm0.1\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}},$ ) is also higher than that of MFM- ${}^{\cdot300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})}$ $(24{-}26\pm0.1\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-}}^{1}$ ). Finally, the $\varrho_{\mathrm{st}}$ values of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ are similar for both compounds and within the range of $17{-}19\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ .
For unsaturated hydrocarbons (i.e., $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}^{\cdot}$ ), the uptake capacity and $\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ are much higher in MFM-300(VIII) than in MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ at low pressure, indicating the presence of possible - $\scriptstyle\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}\equiv\mathbf{C}$ interactions. In comparison, for saturated hydrocarbons (i.e., ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ), the $\varrho_{\mathrm{st}}$ in both compounds are similar, suggesting the presence of similar host-guest binding mechanisms for saturated hydrocarbons in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ and $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ . The observed undulations in the $Q_{s t}$ plots may suggest a packing effect reflecting optimum packing of the substrate within the pore at specific loadings.
## Determination of preferred binding sites via neutron powder diffraction (NPD)
In order to determine the preferred binding sites for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{C_{2}D_{6}}$ and $\mathrm{CD}_{4}$ in MFM-300(V), NPD experiments were carried out for gas-loaded samples of $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ and $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ at $7\mathrm{~K~}$ . Comparison of the NPD patterns for bare and gas-loaded MOFs indicated that no major structural phase change took place on gas-loading, and NPD data enabled full structural analysis via Rietveld refinement to yield positions, orientations and occupancies of adsorbed gas molecules within the framework hosts.
At a loading of $1.0\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{V},$ , analysis of the NPD data revealed two crystallographically independent   
sites for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ (I and II) (Fig. 4a,c). $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{D}_{2}^{\mathrm{~I~}}$ is in a side-on mode interacting with the hydroxyl group with a   
$\operatorname{H}_{\mathrm{OH}}\cdots\cdot\mathbf{c}.\mathbf{g}._{\mathrm{C\equivC}}$ distance of 3.016(1) $\mathrm{\AA}$ (c.g. $\mathbf{\Sigma}=$ centre of gravity), indicating a moderate hydrogen bond between   
the $\pi$ -electrons of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ and the HO–V moiety. $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}^{\mathrm{~II}}$ interacts with the carboxylate O atoms with a $\mathbf{D}{\cdots}{\mathbf{0}}$   
distance of 2.608(1) and 2.871(1) Å. The $\operatorname{H}_{\mathrm{OH}}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathbf{c}.\mathbf{g}._{\mathrm{C\equivC}}$ distance is shorter than that found in the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ -loaded   
MFM-300(Al) [3.26(1) Å] studied by NPD at $10\mathrm{K}$ .8 The stronger hydrogen bond observed in MFM-300(VIII)   
is likely attributed to the difference in the acidity between the Al-OH and V-OH bridges, and is consistent 7
with the observation in the corresponding $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -loaded MFM-300 systems where adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binds to V-OH groups more strongly than to the Al-OH group. $^{10,17}\Pi^{\cdots}\pi$ bonding is observed between the $\pi$ orbitals of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{D}_{2}^{\mathrm{~I~}}$ and the aromatic benzene rings in the carboxylate ligand, with a c.g.C≡C···c.g.phenyl distance of 3.591(2) Å. Further dipole interactions are observed between adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ molecules on sites I and II in a T-shape arrangement with a $\scriptstyle\mathrm{D\cdotsc.g.}_{\mathrm{C=C}}$ distance of 3.190(1) $\textrm{\AA}$ (Figs. S10, S11), comparable to that in solid $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}\ [H^{\cdot\cdot\cdot}c.g.c.c=3.178(1)\ \AA]}$ .18 The site occupancy of adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ molecules was obtained by free refinement against the NPD data. The occupancies of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{D}_{2}^{\mathrm{~I~}}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}^{\mathrm{~II}}$ are found to be 0.55 and 0.45, respectively, at a loading of $1.0\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{V},$ indicating that site I which contacts to the hydroxyl group directly is more favourable at low pressure. As the loading increases to $1.76~\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{V},$ the occupancies of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{D}_{2}^{\mathrm{~I~}}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}^{\mathrm{~II}}$ are found to be 0.82 and 0.94, respectively, indicating the presence of intermolecular cooperative binding between adsorbed gas molecules.
Analysis of the NPD data of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ -loaded MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ also shows two independent binding sites (I’, II’) for adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ molecules with retention of the framework structure. $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{D}_{2}^{\mathrm{~I~}^{\prime}}$ interacts with the carboxylate group with $\mathbf{D}{\cdots}{\mathrm{O}}_{\mathrm{COO}}$ distances of 2.34(1) and 2.43(1) Å, which are slightly longer than the $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{C}2\mathrm{H}2}.\cdot\cdot\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{COO}}$ hydrogen bond distance (2.2 Å) observed in $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(p z d c)_{2}(p y z)]}$ at 170 K (pzdc2- = pyrazine-2,3-dicarboxylate, pyz $\mathbf{\tau}=$ pyrazine),19 but are shorter than the corresponding $\mathbf{D}{\cdots}{\mathbf{O}}_{\mathrm{COO}}$ separation of 3.12(1) $\textrm{\AA}$ found in $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ -loaded MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ . This indicates a shift in the nature of the primary binding site on going from $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ -loaded MFM-300(VIII) to $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ -loaded MFM-300(VIV) (Figs. 4a,b). Π···π interactions between $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{D}_{2}^{\mathrm{~I~}^{\prime}}$ and the phenyl groups are also observed with a c.g.C≡C···c.g.phenyl distance of 3.78(1) Å. The shortest separation between the adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ molecules and the bridging oxy group is 4.83(1) Å.
Acetylene or propyne are frequently used as probes to determine the basicity of oxide materials (e.g., $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{MgO}_{\mathcal{I}}^{\mathrm{{\prime}}}}\end{array}$ ) by forming hydrogen bonds between the acidic C-H groups and the basic sites on the material surface.
In this study, the absence of any notable binding interactions between adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ molecules and the surface oxy groups is a direct result of the weak basicity of the oxy bridges in MFM- ${300}(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ , which, in turn, reflects the relative acidity of the hydroxyl bridges in MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ , consistent with the binding observed in $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ -loaded MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ . In contrast to $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ -loaded MFM-300(VIII), $\pi^{\cdot\cdot\cdot}\pi$ interactions are observed between the adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}^{\mathrm{~I~}^{\prime}}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}^{\mathrm{\scriptsize~II^{\circ}}}$ molecules in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ with a c.g. $\Gamma^{\prime}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathrm{c.g.}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ’ distance of 3.45(2) Å (Figs. S10, S11). Refinement of the site occupancies shows a 50:50 distribution over the two sites, and no preferred binding sites for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ .
The packing of adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ molecules within the two frameworks is compared in Figures 4e-f. Overall, the host-guest interaction is stronger in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ while the guest-guest interaction is stronger in MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ . This result suggests that the proton on the bridging oxy group can not only affect the host-guest binding through fine-turning of the pore surface chemistry, but also alter the subsequent guest-guest interaction, thus controlling the gas adsorption and substrate packing within the pores.
The structures for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ -loaded MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ and $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ have also been optimised by DFT calculations at a loading of $2.0\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{V}$ (Fig. 5). The DFT calculation for MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ confirms that the adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules (i) interact with the hydroxyl groups in a side-on model $(\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{OH}}{\cdot\cdot\cdot}\mathrm{c}.\mathrm{g}._{\mathrm{C}\equiv\mathrm{C}}=2.56\ \mathrm{\AA})$ ; (ii) form $\pi^{\cdot\cdot\cdot}\pi$ bonds with the phenyl rings of ligands $({\tt c.g.c=c\cdot\mathrm{\cdot\cdot\mathrm{c.g._{phenyl}=3.86~\mathrm{\AA}}}})$ ; (iii) bind with the carboxylate oxygen atom via dipole interactions $(\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{C2H2}}{\cdot\cdot\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{carboxylate}}=2.88\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . In contrast, the DFT calculation for $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ confirms (i) an absence of binding interaction between the adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules and the bridging oxy group; (ii) presence of $\pi^{\cdot\cdot\cdot}\pi$ interaction between $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules and the phenyl rings of ligands $({\tt c.g.c=c\cdot\cdot\cdot c.g._{p h e n y l}=3.89\AA})$ ; (iii) the adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules interact strongly with the carboxylate oxygen atom $(\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{C2H2}}{\cdot\cdot\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{carboxylate}}=2.77\mathrm{~\AA})$ . The shift of the binding strength for host-guest and guest-guest interaction on going from $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}}){\cdot}4\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}}){\cdot}4\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was clearly observed in the DFT calculation (Figs. 4c-d), and is in excellent agreement with the results from NPD
experiments.
$\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}}){\cdot}2.12\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ shows two binding domains for $\mathrm{C_{2}D_{4}}$ (Figs. 3g, S12). $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{4}^{\mathrm{~I~}}$ (occupancy $\mathbf{\Sigma}=$ 0.56) is located close to the hydroxyl group with a $\mathsf{c.g.c=c\cdots H_{O H}}$ distance of 3.737(1) Å, which is much longer than the corresponding value of 3.016(1) Å observed in $\mathrm{\sfMFM-300(V^{III})}{\cdot}2.0\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ . This result suggests the presence of a weaker hydrogen bond due to the reduced $\pi$ -electron density in $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{4}$ compared to $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ . $\mathrm{C_{2}D_{4}^{\mathrm{~II}}}$ molecules (occupancy $=0.50\dot{}$ ) contacts with the carboxylate O atoms with D···Ocarboxylate distances in the range of 2.64(1)-3.03(2) Å. The NPD analysis of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{6}$ -loaded MFM-300(VIII) suggests two sites for adsorbed $\mathrm{C_{2}D_{6}}$ molecules, $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{6}^{\mathrm{~I~}}$ molecule is aligned at a long distance to the –OH group [distance $\mathrm{c.g.c.c^{\cdot\cdot\cdot}H_{O H}}=3.87(1)\mathrm{\AA}]$ as a result of no $\pi$ -electron density and notable repulsion between the host and guest hydrogen atoms (Figs. 3d,h, S13). $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{6}^{\mathrm{\scriptsize~II}}$ interacts with the framework and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{6}^{\mathrm{~I~}}$ molecule via van der Waals interactions. Similarly, NPD result of $\mathrm{CD}_{4}$ -loaded MFM-300(VIII) confirms no specific host-guest interactions but van der Waals interactions between adsorbed $\mathrm{CD}_{4}$ molecules and the MOF interior [distance $\mathrm{c.g.c.c}\cdot\mathrm{\cdot\cdotH_{OH}}=3.59(2)\mathrm{\AA}](\mathrm{Figs.\mathrm{\thinspaceS14,S15}}).$
Combining the results of NPD experiments and DFT calculations, the differences in measured $\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ between the two materials can be explained. (i) The $\varrho_{\mathrm{st}}$ values for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ are higher in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ than those in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ at low surface coverage, which is due to the stronger binding interaction between adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ molecules with the framework host of $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ , particularly with the free acidic hydroxyl groups via hydrogen bonds. (ii) The $\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ increases with increasing gas loading in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ , while that for $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ remains near-constant over the entire loading, which is due to the stronger intermolecular interaction between adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules within the pore of MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ as a result of lack of specific surface binding sites. (iii) The $\varrho_{\mathrm{st}}$ values of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ are similar for both MFM-300( ${\mathrm{V}}^{[{\mathrm{III}})}$ and MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ because of the absence of host-guest hydrogen bonds via the hydroxyl groups and the van der Waals interaction drives the adsorption of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and
$\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in both materials.
## Inelastic neutron scattering (INS) and DFT modelling
In addition to static crystallographic studies, we have combined INS and DFT to directly visualise the binding dynamics of adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ (Fig. 6). INS is a powerful neutron spectroscopic technique which is particularly sensitive to the dynamics of hydrogen atoms owing to the exceptionally large cross section for that isotope. The INS spectra of condensed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ in the solid state were also collected for comparison. The INS spectrum for bare $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ shows a series of peaks in the range of $40{\cdot}60\ \mathrm{meV}$ attributed to the deformational modes of the hydroxyl group, and the disappearance of these peaks in the INS spectrum of MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ unambiguously confirms the deprotonation of the bridging hydroxyl group upon oxidation.
Comparison of INS spectra, measured at temperatures below $11(\pm0.1)\$ K to minimize the thermal motion of the adsorbed hydrocarbons and the host, reveals five major changes in peak intensity on going from bare MFM-300(VIII) to $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}}){\cdot}3\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (Fig. 6a). Peaks I and II occur at a low energy transfer (5–24 and $25\mathrm{-}50~\mathrm{meV},$ respectively) and peaks III, IV and V at high energy transfer (79, 94 and $113\mathrm{~meV},$ respectively). The difference spectra were obtained by subtracting the spectrum of bare MOF from that of gas-loaded MOFs, and are shown in Fig. 6e together with the spectrum of condensed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in solid state. To understand these changes, DFT calculations were used to simulate the INS spectra and optimise the structures of bare and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -loaded $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ . On convergence, excellent agreement between the calculated and experimental structural models were obtained (Fig. 6c,d). Peaks I, III and IV can be assigned to translational, asymmetric and symmetric C–H vibrational motions of adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules, respectively.8 Peak II can be assigned to the –OH groups bending/wagging perpendicular to the $\mathrm{\DeltaV^{III}-O{-}V^{I I I}}$ plane and peak $\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ to the bending and out-of-plane wagging of the aromatic C–H groups on two benzene rings (Fig. 6e). The significant changes observed for peaks II suggest that adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules have a direct interaction with the –OH groups, and thereby affect their molecular motions and induce the changes in the INS spectra.
In contrast, addition of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ is accompanied by the appearance of four new peaks in the INS spectrum (Fig. 6b). Peaks ${\mathrm{I}}^{\prime}$ , III’, and IV’ can be assigned to translational, asymmetric and symmetric $\mathrm{C-H}$ vibrational motions of adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules, respectively, and peak V’ to the bending and out-of-plane wagging of the aromatic $\mathrm{C-H}$ groups on the benzene rings, which are similar to the observation of MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ . No major changes in the intensity at $40{\cdot}70\ \mathrm{meV}$ , which is in a sharp contrast to that in the INS study of MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ (Fig. 6e).
In the case of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ -loaded MFM-300(VIII), small changes in intensity of peaks at 47 and $115\mathrm{meV}$ were observed upon addition of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , which are attributed to the interaction of adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ with the hydroxyl and $\mathrm{C-H}$ groups from phenyl rings, respectively (Figs. 6e, S17). From the difference INS plot of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ -loaded $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ , it is clear that all peaks are ascribed to the adsorbed ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ molecules, and no specific host-guest interaction was seen (Fig. 6e, S18). Similarly, addition of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ does not induce an obvious change in the INS spectra either (Fig. S19). These results confirm near solid-type motion of adsorbed ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gases within the pore of MFM- $300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ , in excellent agreement with the NPD study and the $\varrho_{\mathrm{st}}$ analysis.
Thus, the INS and DFT results confirm that (i) in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ , both $-\mathrm{OH}$ and –CH/phenyl ring are active binding sites for adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules; (ii) in MFM-300(VIV), $-\mathrm{CH}/$ phenyl ring is the sole binding sites for adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules via formation of supramolecular contacts. These observations are entirely consistent with the NPD studies and confirm the significant effects of bridging hydroxyl groups on the guest binding in the host on a molecular level.
## Conclusions
We report the adsorption of light hydrocarbons in a pair of iso-structural MOFs, MFM-300(VIII/IV), incorporating redox-active vanadium centers. The oxidation of $\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ centers from III to IV induces deprotonation of the bridging hydroxyl group, achieving fine-tuning of the pore environment. It has been confirmed in this study that these protons play a key role in adsorption of unsaturated hydrocarbons (both uptakes and isosteric heats of adsorption). A comprehensive study combining NPD, INS and DFT modelling has unambiguously determined the preferred binding sites and structural dynamics for these host-guest systems. The differences in $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{n}/C H_{4}}$ $(\mathfrak{n}=2,4,6)$ ) selectivities between MFM-300(VIII) and MFM-300(VIV) are fully rationalised. The acidic bridging hydroxyl groups in the pore provides specific binding activity to unsaturated hydrocarbons via formation of hydrogen bonds, leading to both high selectivity and packing efficiency of adsorbed gas molecules.
